od” was just missing — it wasn’t being actively poisoned or destroyed by an ongoing disease. ### Why that doesn’t work for HIV or cancer Now let’s apply that to the diseases we talked about: - **HIV:** After a transfusion, all the new, uninfected blood cells would immediately be exposed to the virus still hiding in lymph nodes, gut, brain, and other tissues. Those tissues would release fresh virus that infects the new cells. It would be like giving a clean bucket of water to someone living in a room where invisible viruses are constantly being sprayed from the walls — the water would be contaminated again almost instantly. In your uncle’s case, there was no such “sprayer” once the bleeding stopped. - **Cancer:** If it’s a blood cancer like leukemia, the problem is a sick bone marrow that keeps pumping out cancer cells. A transfusion would temporarily replace the cells floating in the blood, but the cancerous factory would keep churning out more. For a solid tumor, the main lump isn’t even in the blood — it’s in an organ. A few cancer cells might be circulating, but the tumor stays put, untouched by a transfusion. In other words, your uncle had a **deficiency** that could be topped up. HIV and cancer involve an **active, ongoing destructive process** that continues even after a fresh blood supply is given. ### But didn’t we “swap out” his blood? A typical transfusion for anemia is not an exchange transfusion (where we take out old blood and put in new). It’s a “top-up” — we add donor red cells to his existing blood. His plasma, white cells, and platelets were still his own. So it wasn’t even a full replacement. Even if it had been a complete exchange, the same principles would apply: the virus would still be lurking in tissues, and the cancer factory would still be running. ### A helpful analogy Imagine your car runs out of fuel. You refill it, and it drives fine. That doesn’t mean that refilling fuel “cures” all car problems — it just fixes the low-fuel problem. If 