m (and some sequestered in small vessels, but still reachable). So if you remove a large fraction of those red cells, you are directly removing the parasites themselves, giving the drugs a massive head start. The remaining few parasites are then killed by antimalarials. The swap doesn’t cure by itself, but it reduces the enemy army so the drugs can win. - **HIV: The virus lives deep in tissues, integrated into your own DNA.** HIV does not float freely; it infects CD4 T cells and integrates its genetic code into the cell’s nucleus. Many of those infected cells leave the bloodstream and reside in lymph nodes, the gut, the brain, and other tissues as “reservoirs.” A blood swap replaces only the cells and plasma circulating at that moment. It does not touch the reservoir cells in the tissues. Those cells will immediately produce new virus that infects the new blood. Worse, some of those reservoir cells are long-lived and can reactivate after years. For malaria, there’s no such reservoir; the enemy is all on the battlefield. For HIV, the enemy has military bases deep in the mountains that a blood swap never reaches. - **Cancer: The source is a solid tumor or a cancerous marrow outside the reach of a simple exchange.** In blood cancers, the malignant cells are produced in the bone marrow and spill into the blood. An exchange might temporarily reduce the circulating cells, but the marrow — the factory — keeps pumping them out within hours. In solid tumors, the mass isn’t in the blood at all. No amount of blood swapping removes a lung tumor. Malaria has no equivalent of a marrow factory or a solid organ mass; the parasites are purely in the cells you can siphon out. --- ### The crucial biological difference nobody has mentioned yet This is the key you’ve been driving us toward: - **Malaria parasites are *obligate intraerythrocytic pathogens* — they are only inside red blood cells.** Red blood cells have no nucleus, are replaced constantly, and can be physically filtered or 